# Rank 1725 / CVE-2026-23737: seroval < 1.4.1 rebuilt a serialized node
# tree in packages/seroval/src/core/context/deserializer.ts, and the names
# the payload supplies resolve straight into the deserializer's
# fixed record tables. The switch in `deserialize` returns
# `CONSTANT_VAL[node.s]` and `SYMBOL_REF[node.s]`, and `deserializeError`
# selects its constructor with `ERROR_CONSTRUCTOR[node.s]` before
# `new construct(deserializeString(node.m))`. No lookup checks that the
# name is the record's own property, so a key the table does not define
# still resolves through the record's prototype chain -- the advisory's
# "overriding constant value and error deserialization"
# (GHSA-3rxj-6cgf-8cfw, CWE-502), which is what hands back non-table
# values (a `constructor` key reaches the `Object` function) and with
# them indirect access to JS evaluation. The fixed revision, bundled in the
# 1.4.1/1.4.2 release commit ce9408ebc87312fcad345a73c172212f2a798060,
# routes all three lookups through `deserializeKnownValue`, which returns
# `record[key]` only after `Object.hasOwn(record, key)` and otherwise
# raises `SerovalMalformedNodeError`.
#
# The same release hardens the property-write arm of the same file:
# `deserializeProperties` wrote `result[deserializeString(key)] = value`
# with no key validation at all; the fixed revision routes string keys through
# `isValidKey` (denying `__proto__`, `constructor`, `prototype` and the
# dunder accessors) and stores denied names with `Object.defineProperty`.
# That arm is detected: on the vulnerable revision VYQL-INJ-013
# reports the dynamic write at deserializer.ts:240 and :242 with
# `code.ExternalEntryInput` on the serialized fields as source, and on
# the patched revision the string-key write inside `assignStringProperty`
# is suppressed by the existing `definePropertyPrototypeKeyGuard`
# control binding. Measured on both revisions of the real checkout,
# `timeout 180 ./bin/vyql scan --cache off --max-ram 6GB`, the
# deserializer's VYQL-INJ-013 rows go 2 -> 0 across the changed lines;
# the one row remaining on the patched revision is the symbol branch
# `object[actual] = ...` at :338, which sits behind `isValidSymbol` and
# which no prototype-sensitive string name can reach. Tests (c) and (d)
# pin that live half.
#
# ATTENTION, vocabulary gap, for the arm the advisory names.
#
# 1. Nothing on the lookup path is judged. `vyql match` on the vulnerable
#    deserializer.ts labels `code.ExternalEntryInput` on the params and
#    nothing else in `deserialize` or `deserializeError`; the
#    `ERROR_CONSTRUCTOR[node.s]` lookups and the `new construct(...)`
#    invocation carry no label of any kind. `code.ReflectionInvoke`
#    (VYQL-DESER-003) is the sink concept for invoking a reflected
#    constructor and has a Java emitter only.
# 2. The taint engine has the path. `vyql graph` on the
#    vulnerable file shows deserializeError#param#node ->
#    Attr(node.s) -> Subscript(ERROR_CONSTRUCTOR.__subscript) ->
#    Call(construct), so only the sink label is missing.
# 3. The binding vocabulary cannot identify the call. A JavaScript call
#    node's only callee facts are `callee.path`/`callee.method`, and
#    `new construct(...)` lowers to the same single-segment path as
#    `new Point(...)` where `Point` is a class defined in the same file:
#    no clause can say "this invocation's callee is selected by a
#    computed key", and JavaScript function contexts emit no fact tying a
#    call to the local whose value it invokes -- the recorded
#    no-call-argument-tokens gap (1360), callee side. The two snippets
#    differ in no node, path or token a binding can read.
# 4. The only expressible proxy is ordinary code. A function-scoped
#    presence binding on `indexKey contains "."` (a computed key read
#    whose key is another object's field) with no own-property guard, no
#    prototype guard and no `Object.create` labels exactly the two
#    vulnerable functions on the vulnerable revision, nothing in the
#    patched deserializer -- and 24 more places on this repository's own
#    patched revision: 4 production lookups shaped like
#    `OBJECT_FLAG_CONSTRUCTOR[flag.type]` in serializer.ts's
#    `resolveFlags`, and 20 in test files. Enumerated across the ~170
#    sibling clones under /tmp/cve-work/rank*/, 6,121 JavaScript and
#    TypeScript files carry that shape with no guard, out of 9,004 files
#    (124,563 raw occurrences) containing the computed-read form at all.
#    It is registry dispatch, not a weakness class, and with no join fact
#    (point 3) the shape cannot be tightened below that.
# 5. The fixed revision's own vocabulary is not a discriminator. The patched form's
#    `deserializeKnownValue` and its `Object.hasOwn(record, key)` live in
#    the helper; a negation keyed on the helper's name is the rejected
#    layout-keyed form. What separates the revisions structurally is the
#    own-property check itself, which the `own_property_key_guard` fact
#    does express -- but that fact without the lookup-to-invocation join
#    is the 6,121-file shape of point 4.
#
# What the vocabulary would need, per ENGINE-GAPS.md: a callee-provenance
# fact for JavaScript call nodes -- the local or parameter whose value
# the invocation consumes, or an assign-to-callee join in the binding
# matcher -- so a sink binding can label an invocation whose constructor
# is resolved by a computed key off a param-crossing record. The taint
# path is there; only the sink identification is not expressible.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-vocabulary-gap, both revisions rejected.

test "rank1725: (a) the deserializer resolves serialized names into constant and constructor tables with no own-property check"
  lang javascript
  profile library
  file deserializer.ts
  reject VYQL-INJ-167
  reject VYQL-DESER-003
  code
  ```
  const CONSTANT_VAL: Record<string, unknown> = {};
  const ERROR_CONSTRUCTOR: Record<string, ErrorConstructor> = {};

  export function deserialize(node: any): unknown {
    switch (node.t) {
      case 0:
        return CONSTANT_VAL[node.s];
      case 6:
        return deserializeError(node);
      default:
        return undefined;
    }
  }

  function deserializeError(node: any): Error {
    const construct = ERROR_CONSTRUCTOR[node.s];
    return assignIndexedValue(node.i, new construct(node.m));
  }

  function assignIndexedValue(id: number, value: unknown): unknown {
    return value;
  }
  ```

test "rank1725: (b) the patched deserializer resolves table names through an own-property check"
  lang javascript
  profile library
  file deserializer.ts
  reject VYQL-INJ-167
  reject VYQL-DESER-003
  code
  ```
  const CONSTANT_VAL: Record<string, unknown> = {};

  function deserializeKnownValue<T>(node: unknown, record: Record<string, T>, key: string): T {
    if (Object.hasOwn(record, key)) {
      return record[key];
    }
    throw new MalformedNodeError(node);
  }

  export function deserialize(node: any): unknown {
    switch (node.t) {
      case 0:
        return deserializeKnownValue(node, CONSTANT_VAL, node.s);
      default:
        return undefined;
    }
  }
  ```

test "rank1725: (c) the unvalidated property write keyed by the serialized record is reported"
  lang javascript
  profile library
  file deserializer.ts
  expect VYQL-INJ-013
  code
  ```
  function deserializeString(current: string): string {
    return current;
  }

  export function deserializeProperties(record: any): Record<string, unknown> {
    const result: Record<string, unknown> = {};
    const len = record.s;
    const keys = record.k;
    const vals = record.v;
    for (let i = 0, key: unknown; i < len; i++) {
      key = keys[i];
      if (typeof key === 'string') {
        result[deserializeString(key)] = vals[i];
      }
    }
    return result;
  }
  ```

test "rank1725: (d) the key-checked write with a defineProperty fallback is not reported"
  lang javascript
  profile library
  file deserializer.ts
  reject VYQL-INJ-013
  code
  ```
  function isValidKey(key: string): boolean {
    switch (key) {
      case 'constructor':
      case '__proto__':
      case 'prototype':
        return false;
      default:
        return true;
    }
  }

  function assignStringProperty(
    object: Record<string, unknown>,
    key: string,
    value: unknown,
  ): void {
    if (isValidKey(key)) {
      object[key] = value;
    } else {
      Object.defineProperty(object, key, {
        value,
        configurable: true,
        enumerable: true,
        writable: true,
      });
    }
  }

  function assignProperty(
    object: Record<string, unknown>,
    key: unknown,
    value: unknown,
  ): void {
    if (typeof key === 'string') {
      assignStringProperty(object, key, value);
    }
  }

  export function deserializeProperties(record: any): Record<string, unknown> {
    const result: Record<string, unknown> = {};
    const keys = record.k;
    const vals = record.v;
    for (let i = 0; i < keys.length; i++) {
      assignProperty(result, keys[i], vals[i]);
    }
    return result;
  }
  ```
